BICoE scope – MQS Release 1.3 performance testing
Validate normal MQS traffic + Blob replay traffic together at production-like volume.
Confirm BICoE Event Hub can receive the replayed messages without message loss, abnormal lag or backlog.
Confirm replay does not introduce duplicates.
Confirm only BICoE's expected message types are received during replay. In particular, core_request, which is intended for the Workday flow, should not be populated into the BICoE Event Hub during reprocessing.
Validate that the replayed messages can continue through the BICoE ingestion pipeline without failures or abnormal processing delay.
After the load/replay finishes, confirm the pipeline catches up and returns to its normal processing state.
What the BICoE developer should monitor

During the performance run, I would ask the BICoE developer to capture:

Event Hub: incoming message volume/rate, consumer lag/backlog, throttling/errors and whether throughput changes significantly when Blob replay starts.

BICoE ingestion: processing throughput, failed/rejected messages, exceptions, retry behaviour, processing latency and whether the streaming jobs remain healthy.

Data validation: counts before/during/after replay, duplicate message IDs/quote IDs, missing expected replay samples, and a breakdown by message type to prove that unwanted types such as core_request have not entered BICoE.

Recovery: once replay stops, how long BICoE takes to clear any accumulated backlog and return to its normal processing level.
